A||N||9||Ozzy||Problems with the "Iron Dupe"||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||01-16-2000||03:51 AM||||Hi Gang...<P>I have not even touched my coupe in weeks. I finally drove it yesterday so it's time to re visit the problems I was having the last time I posted.<P>Now lets see...At first the problem was when driving down the road, the engine would sputter some and then the service engine light would come on and the engine would smoothe out. But if I backed off on the accelerator the light would go out and the engine would start running like crap again until the serv. eng. light comes on again and it runs smooth again. It also now will do this when idling unlike before. I don't know what the code is but I'm willing to bet it is the same as before. (I will dig up my old post) This was an intermittent problem too. Not any more! Don't forget ladies...'88 DIS 2.5l Iron Dupe!!! (Codes were 33,34 & 44)<P>I have looked for obvious vacuum leaks, swapped the MAP sensor, the TPS is new, has a new fuel filter. I did dick around with the IAC and the fuel regulator some time ago but I would think that would have created problems long before now. What kind of problem is this? It won't accelerate cleanly and apparently the ECM won't operate the engine on sensor info. I assume this 'cause it feels like the default mixture set by the ECM is kicking in when the Serv. eng. light comes on, not to mention it uses about 3 times the ammount of fuel it normally does.<P>I was gonna dump this chump. What with a kid on the way and trying to buy a house and such. But I have not driven it for awhile and jumped in it yesterday for the hell of it. Goddamnit I luv this little car...I just wish I could get the bastard to run right.<P>Anyway, enough bleating from me. Any thoughts appreciated as usual.<P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 01-16-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||862M4inCA||01-16-2000||11:12 AM||||Well according to the code lists I have, it's a MAP and lean air problem. You could try disconnecting the MAP sensor and see if that improves the way the car runs. With the MAP sensor out of the picture the ECM should run using default values based on info from your other sensors. Otherwise I'd check for vacuum leaks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-16-2000||11:12 AM||||as usual...... check all the ground straps. The single wire O2 sensor uses one of them as part of its circuit. there are grounds to the head, and the bell housing.<P>also check the vac line to the map sensor. for damage/blockage and make sure it's on the right port of the TBI. (It matters what port it's on) <P>disconnect and plug both lines to the fuel canister. it can leak internally.<P>Check teh PCV line elbow below the TBI. It may need to be clamped to the manifold to make sure it doesn't leak. clamp it only tight enough to seal. don't crush it.<P>If it still looks like a fuel problem....88 motors use the 700 series TBI which is very simple to work on. Get the rebuilbd kit for it. You could have a leak in the gasket under the TBI. especialy if it's an automatic tranny. That will also replace the IAC O rings, the fuel line rings, the Fuel Pressure regulator diaphragm, the injector screens, and the gasket under the vac port block.<P>What you really need is an ECM scanner. Then you can watch exactly what is going on when this happens.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||01-16-2000||02:46 PM||||Hey Ogre...thnx again. Do you know of a manufacturer that sells a GOOD TBI rebuild kit? I've been thinking about doing that anyway.<P>The engine will idle clean and rev clean sitting still. When under a load it starts acting up. (5 speed BTW) I also changed the crank sensor recently and had the ign. mod. checked at NAPA. The coil packs were ohmed which obviously does not tell all but it was running fine so, I guess this is some kind of fuel mixture problem huh?<P>Ogre, I know you purchased a scanner not too long back and keep reccomending to every  one to get one. If I recall, it was around $150.00??? What make and model? Is it going to save me time and money in the long run? I'm not looking for miracles I guess but I can see that these trouble codes from the ECM are vague at best. So at least if a scanner will narrow down the possibilities of what to fix to get to the real problem it would be worth it.<P><BR>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-16-2000||05:53 PM||||The scanner I have is AutoXray's GM only unit. It is similar to their XP240, but is only for specific GM cars. It runs 150-180$us depending on where you get it. ((The XP240 with GM cable is about 300$us)) It does help cuite often.<P>Borg Warner makes a good kit. Pep Boys has it. I'm sure others do to. The kit replaces all the gaskets seals and screens. The BWD kit also has the Fuel Reg diaphram. I imagine they all do. Make sure you use the gaskets that match the old ones. The kit covers more than one application. Make sure you lube the O rings with ATF or light motor oil when putting things together. DON'T fool with the static idle adjuster screw. DON'T spray/soak solvent inside the IAC motor. Don't disassemble the trottle butterfly.<P>You'll need a T30 torx, and a 10mm wrench. You need a few smaller ones T10-T20 for the TPS sensor, IAC and vacuum plate. <P>Use 2 wrenches to disconect the fuel lines. Preferably "flare nut" wrenches.<P>You might want to consider replacing the injector. Especially if the car is over 100K miles. Replace the IAC if it's gooked up. ((You can try WD40 to clean the IAC. It won't atack the insulation in the guts of it.))<P>What if any ignition parts have you replaced? A bad plug or wire can really screw up DIS. The coils may Ohm good and still be bad. The motor needs more power under load. If one of the coils insulation is breaking down it can either be leaking spark energy internally, or leak directly to ground. At 25+KV you don't need a dead short for it to draw off considerable energy.<P>If you did the plugs recheck the gap. It has to be very close to .060 on all 4 plugs. if one plug is off it will affect the other plug in the pair.<P>Don't install Bosch Platinum of any type in DIS motors. DIS eats the center electrode out of them. I don't know why, it just does. So far everyone I've heard of that used them in a DIS motor has had problems. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||01-16-2000||08:49 PM||||Hey Ogre...<P>I changed the crank sensor (maybe you remember my last problem of the engine quitting at operating temp???)I also had the ignition module tested by a dude at NAPA auto on his special little simulator. I made him test it about 15 times too. Passed every time. He is the one who buzzed the coils for me. They matched in impedance but ofcourse this was cold and not under load. After I put the new crank sensor in it appeared to run like it's normal old self. It has always had something of a slight hesitation under full acceleration over 3500 rpm but not bad enough to worry about. Would igniton problems constantly give those codes??(Lean exhaust etc)Not to mention the constant kicking in of the Serv. Eng. light which always coincides with the engine running smooth again.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-16-2000||11:15 PM||||Check the vac line to the map. you said you replaced it I think.... If that line is leaking or blocked it could do weird things as load changes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ChadMan||01-17-2000||10:49 AM||||Hey Ozzy, I rebuilt my 88 TBI unit with a kit from Napa and replaced the injector at the same time.  That was 40K miles ago.  The rebuild kit was something like $20 and I think the injector was around $60.  This cured stumbling under acceleration and rough running at high rpms, but I still have an unsteady idle, which I can happily live with.<P>The rebuild is pretty easy, too - much easier than rebuilding even a 1-bbl carburetor.||||reg||1
Z||000008||David Gamache||01-17-2000||06:33 PM||||Remember not to overlook the exhaust manifold. The G.M 2.5L motors are known to crack and cause problems. Check for cracks with a small mirror and a flash light. Look closely around the o2 sensor and factory welds. If a crack is located it is possible to have the manifold rewelded if removed from the vehicle, but by a specialist such as can be found at a eighteen wheeler truck repair center. The metal used in the manifold requires a specific type of weld. A muffler shop and such is usually not equiped. Also when the manifold is off remove the heat shield and toss it. The shield serves to increase heat to the manifold and crystalize the metal leading to early failure another G.M bonus. Faulty plug wires have caused a problem like yours as well. A faulty egr may cause a slight power hesitation but does not usually set codes. A bad fuel injector will also cause serious hesitation. Just a few ideas. Hope it helps.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ozzy||01-18-2000||10:38 AM||||I have headers instead of the stock manifold.<BR>Incedently, the codes are 13, 34 & 44. I isolated the fuel vapor canister...no change.<BR>The vac line to the map sensor is fine. I'm wondering if the small vac line manifold on the base of the throttle body might be leaking?? I'm gonna swap the O2 sensor just for the hell of it...I don't think it is the problem but can't hurt. I'm gonna try to get a TBI rebuild kit today along with a new IAC and injector.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
